1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,116
You are the man who

2
00:00:01,116 --> 00:00:02,371
took the captain's coat.

3
00:00:02,371 --> 00:00:03,736
You held out your hand,

4
00:00:03,736 --> 00:00:07,168
he handed it over, and it went into the common chest.

5
00:00:07,168 --> 00:00:08,559
Then you ran the auction

6
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:09,949
where he bought it back.

7
00:00:09,949 --> 00:00:11,778
He has almost forgiven you.

8
00:00:11,778 --> 00:00:12,853
Two episodes ago,

9
00:00:12,853 --> 00:00:15,098
you were Tom, holding a bucket.

10
00:00:15,098 --> 00:00:15,672
Last time,

11
00:00:15,672 --> 00:00:18,141
you were the captain, losing that coat.

12
00:00:18,141 --> 00:00:21,200
This time, you are the quartermaster:

13
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,398
the steady man who signed Tom aboard,

14
00:00:23,398 --> 00:00:26,317
and the one the crew brings its complaints to.

15
00:00:26,317 --> 00:00:28,386
Imagine you are an experienced

16
00:00:28,386 --> 00:00:31,914
seaman on the same modest Caribbean sloop,

17
00:00:31,914 --> 00:00:33,259
around seventeen seventeen.

18
00:00:34,023 --> 00:00:34,362
You,

19
00:00:34,362 --> 00:00:37,438
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

20
00:00:37,812 --> 00:00:39,829
The problems come from trial records,

21
00:00:39,829 --> 00:00:41,634
eyewitness accounts and

22
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:44,182
historians' research on real pirate crews.

23
00:00:44,717 --> 00:00:46,966
And remember what the job was.

24
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:49,887
A quartermaster helped organize robbery,

25
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:51,514
then kept the accounts.

26
00:00:51,514 --> 00:00:53,669
The sailors on the ships you

27
00:00:53,669 --> 00:00:54,809
board are not scenery,

28
00:00:54,809 --> 00:00:58,093
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

29
00:00:58,093 --> 00:01:00,293
Your story starts with a vote.

30
00:01:00,293 --> 00:01:02,923
When the crew removed the last captain,

31
00:01:02,923 --> 00:01:04,174
every office went back to

32
00:01:04,174 --> 00:01:06,640
a show of hands, including yours.

33
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,011
On a pirate ship,

34
00:01:08,011 --> 00:01:10,335
a job was a loan from the majority.

35
00:01:10,335 --> 00:01:12,709
Our old friend Captain Charles Johnson,

36
00:01:12,709 --> 00:01:14,505
whose General History of the

37
00:01:14,505 --> 00:01:16,678
Pyrates appeared in seventeen twenty-four,

38
00:01:16,678 --> 00:01:18,673
describes the quartermaster as a

39
00:01:18,673 --> 00:01:21,650
principal officer "of the men's own choosing."

40
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:23,275
Johnson is a pen name,

41
00:01:23,275 --> 00:01:25,989
and his book mixes fact with invention.

42
00:01:25,989 --> 00:01:28,548
So we will check him against other witnesses,

43
00:01:28,548 --> 00:01:29,673
and when a story

44
00:01:29,673 --> 00:01:32,395
rests on that one book from seventeen twenty-four alone,

45
00:01:32,395 --> 00:01:33,478
we will say so.

46
00:01:33,478 --> 00:01:36,664
One of those witnesses is William Snelgrave,

47
00:01:36,664 --> 00:01:38,307
a slave-trading captain

48
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:40,858
held by pirates in seventeen nineteen.

49
00:01:40,858 --> 00:01:43,744
Crews, he wrote, chose a captain to fight,

50
00:01:43,744 --> 00:01:46,972
and then chose "another principal officer,

51
00:01:46,972 --> 00:01:48,522
whom they call quartermaster."

52
00:01:48,522 --> 00:01:49,907
Court records agree.

53
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:51,942
In Boston in seventeen twenty-four,

54
00:01:51,942 --> 00:01:55,244
a captured quartermaster named John

55
00:01:55,244 --> 00:01:57,546
Rose Archer was asked how he got the job.

56
00:01:57,546 --> 00:01:58,322
The company,

57
00:01:58,322 --> 00:02:01,838
he said, "thought him the fittest man for a quartermaster,

58
00:02:01,838 --> 00:02:03,338
and so chose him."

59
00:02:03,338 --> 00:02:05,042
The economist Peter Leeson calls

60
00:02:05,042 --> 00:02:07,159
this a system of checks and balances.

61
00:02:07,159 --> 00:02:08,068
The captain had

62
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:10,279
absolute command in a fight.

63
00:02:10,279 --> 00:02:12,833
The quartermaster held much of everything else:

64
00:02:12,833 --> 00:02:13,825
provisions,

65
00:02:13,825 --> 00:02:16,395
plunder, quarrels and punishment.

66
00:02:16,395 --> 00:02:18,256
Johnson calls the

67
00:02:18,256 --> 00:02:20,776
quartermaster "a sort of civil magistrate."

68
00:02:20,776 --> 00:02:23,962
In plain words, you are the crew's treasurer,

69
00:02:23,962 --> 00:02:24,399
judge,

70
00:02:24,399 --> 00:02:28,081
storekeeper and complaints office, all in one waistcoat.

71
00:02:28,081 --> 00:02:29,914
The vote goes your way.

72
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:32,229
The new captain shakes your hand,

73
00:02:32,229 --> 00:02:33,208
a little stiffly.

74
00:02:33,208 --> 00:02:35,207
He has just remembered that the hands

75
00:02:35,207 --> 00:02:37,279
that elected him can also remove him.

76
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:39,319
Then he notices they are the

77
00:02:39,319 --> 00:02:40,824
same hands that elected you.

78
00:02:40,824 --> 00:02:42,784
A new captain means new articles,

79
00:02:42,784 --> 00:02:44,946
and the articles are your department.

80
00:02:44,946 --> 00:02:46,836
Leeson notes that crews agreed on

81
00:02:46,836 --> 00:02:48,491
their articles before a voyage,

82
00:02:48,491 --> 00:02:51,507
at the same time as they elected their officers,

83
00:02:51,507 --> 00:02:53,826
and that every man had to consent.

84
00:02:53,826 --> 00:02:55,889
Then everyone swears to them.

85
00:02:55,889 --> 00:02:58,489
Johnson says Bartholomew Roberts's crew

86
00:02:58,489 --> 00:03:01,241
kept a Bible reserved for that purpose only.

87
00:03:01,614 --> 00:03:03,931
But only that one book from seventeen

88
00:03:03,931 --> 00:03:06,638
twenty-four claims that John Phillips's crew,

89
00:03:06,638 --> 00:03:09,655
lacking a Bible, swore "upon a hatchet."

90
00:03:10,172 --> 00:03:11,705
Your crew owns one Bible,

91
00:03:11,705 --> 00:03:14,432
two hatchets and very strong opinions.

92
00:03:14,741 --> 00:03:16,956
The Bible wins the vote, narrowly.

93
00:03:16,956 --> 00:03:19,525
Then you read out the clauses that

94
00:03:19,525 --> 00:03:21,505
make you the most watched man aboard.

95
00:03:21,505 --> 00:03:23,448
In the articles Johnson printed

96
00:03:23,448 --> 00:03:24,614
for Roberts's crew,

97
00:03:24,614 --> 00:03:27,471
the quartermaster received two shares of a prize,

98
00:03:27,471 --> 00:03:29,340
exactly like the captain.

99
00:03:29,906 --> 00:03:33,096
Another clause called every man "fairly in turn,

100
00:03:33,096 --> 00:03:34,738
by list" to board prizes,

101
00:03:34,738 --> 00:03:38,249
because boarders were allowed a change of clothes.

102
00:03:38,249 --> 00:03:40,874
On your sloop, guess who keeps the list.

103
00:03:40,874 --> 00:03:41,803
The same articles

104
00:03:41,803 --> 00:03:43,498
banned gambling for money.

105
00:03:43,936 --> 00:03:45,655
Lights and candles went out at

106
00:03:45,655 --> 00:03:46,597
eight in the evening,

107
00:03:46,597 --> 00:03:50,113
and anyone still thirsty had to drink on the open deck.

108
00:03:50,113 --> 00:03:51,420
Every man kept his

109
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:53,381
pistols and cutlass clean.

110
00:03:53,381 --> 00:03:55,506
Anyone who cheated the company

111
00:03:55,506 --> 00:03:57,347
of a dollar could be marooned.

112
00:03:57,347 --> 00:03:59,868
Phillips's articles worried about fire.

113
00:03:59,868 --> 00:04:03,218
No smoking in the hold without a cap on your pipe,

114
00:04:03,218 --> 00:04:05,240
and no candle without a lantern.

115
00:04:05,775 --> 00:04:07,793
A wooden ship carrying gunpowder

116
00:04:07,793 --> 00:04:10,233
does not forgive careless smokers.

117
00:04:10,832 --> 00:04:13,026
Only Johnson's book prints the articles

118
00:04:13,026 --> 00:04:14,957
of George Lowther's crew,

119
00:04:14,957 --> 00:04:18,298
and they include a rule that might as well carry your name.

120
00:04:18,298 --> 00:04:20,929
Any gold, jewels or silver found

121
00:04:20,929 --> 00:04:23,654
on a prize, worth a piece of eight,

122
00:04:23,654 --> 00:04:25,022
had to be handed to the

123
00:04:25,022 --> 00:04:27,366
quartermaster within twenty-four hours.

124
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:28,985
So now every man aboard

125
00:04:28,985 --> 00:04:31,578
assumes you are hiding something.

126
00:04:32,031 --> 00:04:34,249
Being trusted with everything is the

127
00:04:34,249 --> 00:04:37,190
fastest way to be suspected of everything.

128
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:38,974
Leeson points out that the

129
00:04:38,974 --> 00:04:41,258
articles fenced you in too.

130
00:04:41,258 --> 00:04:43,619
Written shares and written punishments

131
00:04:43,619 --> 00:04:46,364
limited how much was left to your judgment.

132
00:04:46,364 --> 00:04:48,226
And when an article's

133
00:04:48,226 --> 00:04:49,727
meaning was in doubt,

134
00:04:49,727 --> 00:04:53,001
Johnson says, a jury of the crew decided.

135
00:04:53,001 --> 00:04:55,068
Even the judge had a judge.

136
00:04:55,427 --> 00:04:56,756
Then a sail appears.

137
00:04:56,756 --> 00:04:57,832
During the chase,

138
00:04:57,832 --> 00:05:02,458
the captain commands absolutely, as the crew's own rules allow.

139
00:05:02,458 --> 00:05:05,430
You check the boat, the oars and the men.

140
00:05:05,804 --> 00:05:07,239
Your power begins later,

141
00:05:07,239 --> 00:05:10,811
and it begins in the most dangerous place on the water.

142
00:05:10,811 --> 00:05:12,616
Snelgrave wrote that the

143
00:05:12,616 --> 00:05:15,638
quartermaster was "to be the first man in boarding

144
00:05:15,638 --> 00:05:17,338
any ship they shall attack,

145
00:05:17,338 --> 00:05:20,611
or go in the boat on any desperate enterprise."

146
00:05:20,611 --> 00:05:21,587
Johnson agrees:

147
00:05:21,587 --> 00:05:24,697
the quartermaster was first on board any prize.

148
00:05:24,697 --> 00:05:26,362
Pirates hoped a crew would

149
00:05:26,362 --> 00:05:29,039
surrender before anyone climbed aboard.

150
00:05:29,039 --> 00:05:31,842
Leeson describes a deliberate bargain:

151
00:05:31,842 --> 00:05:33,929
mercy for crews that gave up,

152
00:05:33,929 --> 00:05:37,617
and something much worse for those that fought.

153
00:05:37,617 --> 00:05:39,121
Real trial records show

154
00:05:39,121 --> 00:05:41,549
quartermasters in front.

155
00:05:41,549 --> 00:05:44,022
In January seventeen twenty-two,

156
00:05:44,331 --> 00:05:45,351
off West Africa,

157
00:05:45,351 --> 00:05:50,844
Roberts's quartermaster, William Magnes, commanded the boat that took the King Solomon,

158
00:05:50,844 --> 00:05:52,755
a Royal African Company ship.

159
00:05:52,755 --> 00:05:54,765
Witnesses said Magnes ordered

160
00:05:54,765 --> 00:05:56,938
her provisions and stores out.

161
00:05:56,938 --> 00:05:59,371
The boatswain went straight for two new

162
00:05:59,371 --> 00:06:01,066
cables and coils of rope,

163
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:04,987
and beat the merchant sailors for not working fast enough.

164
00:06:04,987 --> 00:06:06,670
Robbery, it turns out,

165
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:09,386
is mostly inventory, with threats.

166
00:06:09,386 --> 00:06:11,273
Your own boarding is the

167
00:06:11,273 --> 00:06:13,130
merchant sloop from last time.

168
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:15,016
You go over the rail first,

169
00:06:15,016 --> 00:06:16,477
with a pistol in your belt that

170
00:06:16,477 --> 00:06:18,238
you very much hope stays there.

171
00:06:18,548 --> 00:06:20,478
Their crew has already surrendered.

172
00:06:20,478 --> 00:06:22,693
A frightened young sailor hands

173
00:06:22,693 --> 00:06:24,789
you the ship's papers with both hands.

174
00:06:24,789 --> 00:06:26,593
Surrender spared lives,

175
00:06:26,593 --> 00:06:28,788
but it did not make the afternoon gentle.

176
00:06:29,339 --> 00:06:31,675
Snelgrave and the victims who

177
00:06:31,675 --> 00:06:34,797
testified in seventeen twenty-two describe threats,

178
00:06:34,797 --> 00:06:37,538
blows and cutlasses waved in faces.

179
00:06:37,993 --> 00:06:39,336
The people on that deck are

180
00:06:39,336 --> 00:06:41,602
afraid of you, and they are right to be.

181
00:06:42,089 --> 00:06:43,535
Then you do what Johnson

182
00:06:43,535 --> 00:06:44,982
says quartermasters did.

183
00:06:44,982 --> 00:06:47,144
You separate what the company will keep,

184
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:47,873
and you return

185
00:06:47,873 --> 00:06:49,825
what you think fit to the owners.

186
00:06:50,264 --> 00:06:52,765
Gold and silver are not returnable.

187
00:06:52,765 --> 00:06:55,437
Your crew voted on that long ago.

188
00:06:55,437 --> 00:06:57,733
The legend imagines a treasure hunter.

189
00:06:57,733 --> 00:06:59,109
The reality is a nervous

190
00:06:59,109 --> 00:07:01,421
warehouse manager on a very small boat.

191
00:07:01,924 --> 00:07:03,369
Leeson notes there was rarely

192
00:07:03,369 --> 00:07:04,767
room aboard a pirate ship to

193
00:07:04,767 --> 00:07:06,854
take everything a prize carried.

194
00:07:06,854 --> 00:07:08,087
You take flour,

195
00:07:08,087 --> 00:07:11,897
rum, rope, sailcloth and a few bolts of cloth.

196
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:13,895
You leave the merchant his salt

197
00:07:13,895 --> 00:07:16,420
fish and enough bread to reach a harbor.

198
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:17,110
You call that

199
00:07:17,110 --> 00:07:18,589
returning what you think fit.

200
00:07:18,589 --> 00:07:20,107
He calls it something else.

201
00:07:20,107 --> 00:07:22,040
Real crews could be wasteful.

202
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:23,850
When pirates decided to keep

203
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:26,951
Snelgrave's own ship, he watched them throw

204
00:07:26,951 --> 00:07:28,624
bales of woollen goods and cases of

205
00:07:28,624 --> 00:07:30,837
Indian cloth into the sea to clear her.

206
00:07:31,258 --> 00:07:32,855
Witnesses at the seventeen

207
00:07:32,855 --> 00:07:35,687
twenty-two trial described Roberts's

208
00:07:35,687 --> 00:07:37,389
quartermasters ordering a captured

209
00:07:37,389 --> 00:07:39,391
ship's goods thrown overboard too.

210
00:07:39,847 --> 00:07:41,190
Money and necessities,

211
00:07:41,190 --> 00:07:44,587
Snelgrave wrote, were what they chiefly wanted.

212
00:07:44,587 --> 00:07:45,923
Plunder that would not

213
00:07:45,923 --> 00:07:47,907
fit simply stopped existing.

214
00:07:48,523 --> 00:07:50,566
Whatever cannot be split goes

215
00:07:50,566 --> 00:07:53,123
into the chest, to be "sold at

216
00:07:53,123 --> 00:07:55,604
the mast," as Snelgrave put it.

217
00:07:55,978 --> 00:07:57,560
Leeson explains that crews

218
00:07:57,560 --> 00:08:00,618
auctioned awkward items and shared the money.

219
00:08:00,618 --> 00:08:01,356
That is how the

220
00:08:01,356 --> 00:08:03,041
captain bought back his coat.

221
00:08:03,463 --> 00:08:05,962
You ran the auction and kept a straight face,

222
00:08:05,962 --> 00:08:07,546
which was the hardest part.

223
00:08:07,546 --> 00:08:09,977
The rest has to become money ashore.

224
00:08:10,592 --> 00:08:12,549
Royal Museums Greenwich notes

225
00:08:12,549 --> 00:08:14,997
that stolen goods were sold in

226
00:08:14,997 --> 00:08:17,281
nearby islands and European colonies.

227
00:08:17,281 --> 00:08:19,391
Johnson describes colonial officials

228
00:08:19,391 --> 00:08:21,891
sharing plunder and trading with pirates.

229
00:08:22,249 --> 00:08:23,635
Your dealer's prices drop the

230
00:08:23,635 --> 00:08:26,086
moment he sees where your cloth came from.

231
00:08:26,086 --> 00:08:27,417
And some prizes carried

232
00:08:27,417 --> 00:08:29,548
people the law treated as cargo.

233
00:08:30,435 --> 00:08:32,606
Royal Museums Greenwich says

234
00:08:32,606 --> 00:08:35,440
pirates rarely freed enslaved people.

235
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:37,164
They often treated them as badly as

236
00:08:37,164 --> 00:08:38,299
their captors did,

237
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:41,589
and usually sold them for profit once they reached land.

238
00:08:41,589 --> 00:08:44,638
At Whydah, on the West African coast,

239
00:08:44,638 --> 00:08:46,283
in January seventeen twenty-two,

240
00:08:46,628 --> 00:08:47,803
Roberts's crew demanded

241
00:08:47,803 --> 00:08:49,768
ransoms from the ships at anchor.

242
00:08:50,159 --> 00:08:51,673
Captain Chaloner Ogle of the

243
00:08:51,673 --> 00:08:54,068
Royal Navy reported that one English ship,

244
00:08:54,068 --> 00:08:55,883
whose captain refused to pay,

245
00:08:55,883 --> 00:08:58,222
was burned with "a considerable

246
00:08:58,222 --> 00:09:00,313
number" of enslaved Africans aboard.

247
00:09:00,313 --> 00:09:01,939
Johnson's book alone puts

248
00:09:01,939 --> 00:09:03,444
the number at about eighty.

249
00:09:03,444 --> 00:09:05,502
No column in a quartermaster's

250
00:09:05,502 --> 00:09:07,284
book tells the truth about that.

251
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:08,580
The people aboard those

252
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:10,665
ships were never anyone's share.

253
00:09:10,665 --> 00:09:11,483
Back at sea,

254
00:09:11,483 --> 00:09:13,814
a quarrel arrives on schedule.

255
00:09:13,814 --> 00:09:14,986
Your crewmate says Tom

256
00:09:14,986 --> 00:09:16,719
pocketed a silver buckle from the

257
00:09:16,719 --> 00:09:18,527
prize instead of handing it in.

258
00:09:18,527 --> 00:09:20,735
Tom says he found it in his own shoe.

259
00:09:20,735 --> 00:09:23,444
Neither story improves as it gets louder.

260
00:09:23,444 --> 00:09:24,738
This is your court.

261
00:09:24,738 --> 00:09:26,583
Johnson says the quartermaster

262
00:09:26,583 --> 00:09:29,804
punished small offences not covered by the articles:

263
00:09:29,804 --> 00:09:31,344
men who were quarrelsome,

264
00:09:31,344 --> 00:09:35,181
who misused prisoners, who plundered beyond his orders,

265
00:09:35,181 --> 00:09:37,318
or who neglected their weapons.

266
00:09:37,318 --> 00:09:38,632
He punished them,

267
00:09:38,632 --> 00:09:41,674
Johnson says, with "drubbing or whipping," and

268
00:09:41,674 --> 00:09:44,156
anyone else who tried it risked

269
00:09:44,156 --> 00:09:46,146
a lashing from the whole company.

270
00:09:46,146 --> 00:09:48,380
You are the only man aboard

271
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:50,461
allowed to hand out a beating.

272
00:09:50,461 --> 00:09:52,648
That is not a privilege.

273
00:09:52,648 --> 00:09:53,732
It is a target.

274
00:09:53,732 --> 00:09:55,972
The articles set limits.

275
00:09:55,972 --> 00:09:57,265
Roberts's crew banned

276
00:09:57,265 --> 00:09:59,387
striking one another on board.

277
00:09:59,761 --> 00:10:01,971
Phillips's articles punished a

278
00:10:01,971 --> 00:10:03,610
blow with "Moses's law":

279
00:10:03,610 --> 00:10:06,826
forty stripes lacking one, on the bare back.

280
00:10:06,826 --> 00:10:08,633
So when your crewmate shoves Tom,

281
00:10:08,633 --> 00:10:11,530
the case becomes more serious than a buckle.

282
00:10:11,530 --> 00:10:13,678
Roberts's articles said quarrels had to

283
00:10:13,678 --> 00:10:16,621
be ended on shore, at sword and pistol.

284
00:10:16,621 --> 00:10:18,796
Johnson describes the routine.

285
00:10:18,796 --> 00:10:20,155
The quartermaster first

286
00:10:20,155 --> 00:10:22,350
tried to reconcile the two men.

287
00:10:22,350 --> 00:10:23,238
If that failed,

288
00:10:23,238 --> 00:10:26,091
he took them ashore, stood them back to back,

289
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,517
and gave the word to turn and fire.

290
00:10:28,517 --> 00:10:30,010
If both pistols missed,

291
00:10:30,010 --> 00:10:32,371
Johnson adds, they took up cutlasses,

292
00:10:32,371 --> 00:10:35,850
and the first man to draw blood was declared the winner.

293
00:10:35,850 --> 00:10:37,132
You would very much

294
00:10:37,132 --> 00:10:38,686
prefer the reconciliation.

295
00:10:38,686 --> 00:10:41,161
A duel costs you one man for certain,

296
00:10:41,161 --> 00:10:42,381
and possibly two.

297
00:10:42,381 --> 00:10:44,685
So you do what the job mostly is.

298
00:10:44,685 --> 00:10:45,798
You listen.

299
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:46,693
The buckle,

300
00:10:46,693 --> 00:10:48,811
it turns out, belongs to the merchant's mate.

301
00:10:48,811 --> 00:10:49,980
It goes into the chest,

302
00:10:49,980 --> 00:10:51,979
which is not the same as going home.

303
00:10:51,979 --> 00:10:53,846
Tom apologizes for his shoe.

304
00:10:54,172 --> 00:10:56,544
Your crewmate apologizes for his hands.

305
00:10:56,544 --> 00:10:59,056
You write both apologies in the book,

306
00:10:59,056 --> 00:11:00,071
because next week they

307
00:11:00,071 --> 00:11:01,482
will remember them differently.

308
00:11:01,482 --> 00:11:04,151
Some quarrels broke whole companies apart.

309
00:11:04,151 --> 00:11:05,718
Edward Low's quartermaster,

310
00:11:05,718 --> 00:11:09,409
Francis Spriggs, was given command of a captured vessel,

311
00:11:09,409 --> 00:11:10,635
and after bad blood

312
00:11:10,635 --> 00:11:12,910
with Low he sailed off with one of the

313
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:15,370
ships and became a captain himself.

314
00:11:15,370 --> 00:11:17,941
Only Johnson's book explains the quarrel,

315
00:11:17,941 --> 00:11:20,276
and it claims it was about justice.

316
00:11:20,276 --> 00:11:22,770
Spriggs wanted a crewman hanged for

317
00:11:22,770 --> 00:11:25,306
killing a man in cold blood, and Low did not.

318
00:11:25,648 --> 00:11:26,999
A quartermaster's verdict

319
00:11:26,999 --> 00:11:28,713
could decide more than a buckle.

320
00:11:28,713 --> 00:11:30,828
It could decide who stayed.

321
00:11:30,828 --> 00:11:33,979
A few weeks later, your crew takes a second sloop,

322
00:11:33,979 --> 00:11:36,167
newer and faster than your own.

323
00:11:36,167 --> 00:11:38,739
The vote takes about four seconds.

324
00:11:38,739 --> 00:11:39,231
Keep her.

325
00:11:39,231 --> 00:11:42,053
Real crews upgraded whenever they could.

326
00:11:42,427 --> 00:11:44,220
Bartholomew Roberts's flagship,

327
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:45,623
the Royal Fortune,

328
00:11:45,623 --> 00:11:49,262
had been the Onslow, a merchant ship his crew captured.

329
00:11:49,572 --> 00:11:51,250
Blackbeard kept the French

330
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:53,355
slave ship La Concorde and gave

331
00:11:53,355 --> 00:11:55,779
her captain a small sloop instead.

332
00:11:55,779 --> 00:11:56,862
At Whydah,

333
00:11:56,862 --> 00:12:00,812
Ogle reported, Roberts's men even carried off a fast

334
00:12:00,812 --> 00:12:03,005
French ship after her ransom had been paid,

335
00:12:03,005 --> 00:12:04,400
planning to fit her out for

336
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:07,017
themselves and quit one of their own.

337
00:12:07,017 --> 00:12:09,472
Two vessels need two commanders.

338
00:12:09,472 --> 00:12:11,498
The captain moves to the new sloop.

339
00:12:11,498 --> 00:12:14,419
The crew votes you command of the old one,

340
00:12:14,419 --> 00:12:15,659
with eight men and a pump

341
00:12:15,659 --> 00:12:17,809
that complains louder than they do.

342
00:12:17,809 --> 00:12:19,598
There were real precedents.

343
00:12:19,598 --> 00:12:21,337
When Charles Vane's crew

344
00:12:21,337 --> 00:12:22,353
voted him out,

345
00:12:22,353 --> 00:12:25,887
his quartermaster, John Rackham, became captain.

346
00:12:25,887 --> 00:12:27,796
And Low gave his quartermaster,

347
00:12:27,796 --> 00:12:28,785
Spriggs,

348
00:12:28,785 --> 00:12:31,790
a captured vessel to command before they parted.

349
00:12:31,790 --> 00:12:33,034
For one week,

350
00:12:33,034 --> 00:12:36,216
you are captain and quartermaster at once.

351
00:12:36,216 --> 00:12:37,921
You give the orders,

352
00:12:37,921 --> 00:12:40,564
and then you receive the complaints about the orders.

353
00:12:40,564 --> 00:12:42,360
Your eight men elect one of

354
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,267
themselves to keep an eye on you.

355
00:12:44,267 --> 00:12:45,184
It is Tom.

356
00:12:45,541 --> 00:12:47,320
He takes it very seriously.

357
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:50,102
Splitting a company had a cost.

358
00:12:50,102 --> 00:12:51,806
In February seventeen twenty-two,

359
00:12:51,806 --> 00:12:54,445
one of Roberts's ships,

360
00:12:54,445 --> 00:12:57,622
the Ranger, left Cape Lopez alone to chase

361
00:12:57,622 --> 00:13:00,065
what her crew took for a merchant ship.

362
00:13:00,065 --> 00:13:01,945
It was the warship Swallow.

363
00:13:01,945 --> 00:13:04,337
The Ranger was taken first.

364
00:13:04,808 --> 00:13:05,766
Five days later,

365
00:13:05,766 --> 00:13:08,257
the Swallow caught Roberts too.

366
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:10,718
Your own week ends quietly.

367
00:13:10,718 --> 00:13:12,616
You rejoin the captain

368
00:13:12,616 --> 00:13:14,293
and hand back the command.

369
00:13:14,293 --> 00:13:15,349
You discover you

370
00:13:15,349 --> 00:13:17,613
enjoyed it slightly too much.

371
00:13:17,613 --> 00:13:18,649
Somewhere aboard,

372
00:13:18,649 --> 00:13:20,867
someone is already discussing that.

373
00:13:20,867 --> 00:13:22,575
Then the dealer brings the

374
00:13:22,575 --> 00:13:24,219
news you heard last time:

375
00:13:24,219 --> 00:13:27,061
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

376
00:13:27,636 --> 00:13:30,222
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

377
00:13:30,222 --> 00:13:30,742
seventeen eighteen,

378
00:13:30,742 --> 00:13:34,783
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

379
00:13:34,783 --> 00:13:36,795
You read the reward list carefully,

380
00:13:36,795 --> 00:13:39,275
because reading carefully is your job.

381
00:13:39,602 --> 00:13:41,389
As the London Gazette printed it,

382
00:13:41,389 --> 00:13:42,335
catching a pirate

383
00:13:42,335 --> 00:13:44,217
commander was worth one hundred pounds.

384
00:13:44,217 --> 00:13:46,202
Every lieutenant,

385
00:13:46,202 --> 00:13:50,258
master, boatswain, carpenter and gunner was worth forty.

386
00:13:50,258 --> 00:13:53,087
Inferior officers were thirty,

387
00:13:53,475 --> 00:13:55,068
and private men twenty.

388
00:13:55,068 --> 00:13:57,669
Quartermasters are not on the list.

389
00:13:57,669 --> 00:13:59,354
The officer who ran a pirate

390
00:13:59,354 --> 00:14:00,982
ship's money and discipline

391
00:14:00,982 --> 00:14:03,219
did not get a line of his own.

392
00:14:03,219 --> 00:14:05,108
You are not sure whether

393
00:14:05,108 --> 00:14:07,186
to feel insulted or relieved.

394
00:14:07,186 --> 00:14:09,001
Relief would be a mistake.

395
00:14:09,001 --> 00:14:11,393
Courts did not need a price list to

396
00:14:11,393 --> 00:14:13,873
understand what a quartermaster was.

397
00:14:13,873 --> 00:14:15,627
In the spring of seventeen twenty-two,

398
00:14:15,627 --> 00:14:19,734
Roberts's captured men were tried at Cape Coast Castle,

399
00:14:19,734 --> 00:14:21,568
on the West African coast.

400
00:14:21,568 --> 00:14:23,699
Prisoners said they had only obeyed

401
00:14:23,699 --> 00:14:25,885
their captain and their quartermaster.

402
00:14:26,370 --> 00:14:28,022
The court answered that orders

403
00:14:28,022 --> 00:14:29,407
from "officers of your own

404
00:14:29,407 --> 00:14:31,864
election" were still their own acts.

405
00:14:31,864 --> 00:14:33,131
The vote that made you

406
00:14:33,131 --> 00:14:35,351
could also convict your voters.

407
00:14:35,351 --> 00:14:36,803
One prisoner protested that

408
00:14:36,803 --> 00:14:39,792
he had never voted for the new quartermaster,

409
00:14:39,792 --> 00:14:40,732
William Magnes.

410
00:14:40,732 --> 00:14:42,806
He had voted for David Simpson,

411
00:14:42,806 --> 00:14:44,222
the old quartermaster,

412
00:14:44,222 --> 00:14:48,216
because Magnes was "too honest a man for the business."

413
00:14:48,216 --> 00:14:49,879
It did not help Magnes.

414
00:14:49,879 --> 00:14:51,354
He and Simpson,

415
00:14:51,354 --> 00:14:55,168
the new quartermaster and the old one, were among the first

416
00:14:55,168 --> 00:14:57,950
six men hanged outside Cape Coast Castle.

417
00:14:58,388 --> 00:14:58,839
In all,

418
00:14:58,839 --> 00:15:00,999
fifty-two of Roberts's men were hanged.

419
00:15:01,533 --> 00:15:03,564
Twenty were sentenced to seven years of

420
00:15:03,564 --> 00:15:06,183
forced labour on the West African coast.

421
00:15:06,183 --> 00:15:08,740
More than seventy were acquitted,

422
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:11,654
most of them men who had been forced aboard.

423
00:15:11,654 --> 00:15:13,694
And around seventy Black men captured

424
00:15:13,694 --> 00:15:16,301
with Roberts's crews were never tried at all.

425
00:15:16,301 --> 00:15:18,602
They were sold into slavery.

426
00:15:18,602 --> 00:15:20,987
Two years later came John Rose Archer,

427
00:15:20,987 --> 00:15:22,106
the man his company

428
00:15:22,106 --> 00:15:24,196
called the fittest for the job.

429
00:15:24,538 --> 00:15:26,103
Witnesses at his Boston trial

430
00:15:26,103 --> 00:15:28,139
said that once he became quartermaster

431
00:15:28,139 --> 00:15:30,018
of John Phillips's crew,

432
00:15:30,018 --> 00:15:32,927
he went armed aboard every vessel they took.

433
00:15:32,927 --> 00:15:35,448
That career ended in April seventeen twenty-four,

434
00:15:35,448 --> 00:15:36,700
when captives

435
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:39,759
aboard rose up and overpowered the crew.

436
00:15:39,759 --> 00:15:42,544
Johnson's book alone claims Archer was

437
00:15:42,544 --> 00:15:44,580
spared that day because he held all the plunder,

438
00:15:44,580 --> 00:15:46,209
which made him useful evidence.

439
00:15:46,551 --> 00:15:48,274
He was hanged in Boston that June,

440
00:15:48,274 --> 00:15:49,805
beside a young shipmate.

441
00:15:50,308 --> 00:15:52,455
Afterwards, the shipmate was buried.

442
00:15:52,455 --> 00:15:54,544
The quartermaster's body was hung in

443
00:15:54,544 --> 00:15:56,360
chains on an island in the harbor,

444
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:58,424
as the Boston Gazette put it,

445
00:15:58,424 --> 00:16:01,187
"to be a spectacle, and so a warning to others."

446
00:16:01,641 --> 00:16:04,321
There was one late mercy for some.

447
00:16:04,321 --> 00:16:06,133
In December seventeen eighteen,

448
00:16:06,133 --> 00:16:08,297
a new proclamation extended the pardon to

449
00:16:08,297 --> 00:16:11,285
pirates who surrendered by the first of July,

450
00:16:11,285 --> 00:16:11,796
seventeen nineteen,

451
00:16:11,796 --> 00:16:15,301
because some had not heard of the first one in time.

452
00:16:15,301 --> 00:16:17,311
The pardon got a second deadline.

453
00:16:17,311 --> 00:16:19,379
The gallows never needed one.

454
00:16:19,753 --> 00:16:21,351
Which brings you back to the deck,

455
00:16:21,351 --> 00:16:22,668
where we left the crew last

456
00:16:22,668 --> 00:16:25,022
time with their hands in the air.

457
00:16:25,022 --> 00:16:26,406
Pardon, or keep sailing?

458
00:16:26,406 --> 00:16:28,253
The captain speaks first.

459
00:16:28,253 --> 00:16:29,044
Then Tom.

460
00:16:29,044 --> 00:16:32,074
Then everybody else, at length.

461
00:16:32,074 --> 00:16:34,108
Counting is your job.

462
00:16:34,108 --> 00:16:35,479
You count once.

463
00:16:35,479 --> 00:16:36,608
You count again,

464
00:16:36,608 --> 00:16:41,347
because whatever number you say, somebody will call it fixed.

465
00:16:41,347 --> 00:16:43,443
The pardon wins, narrowly.

466
00:16:43,977 --> 00:16:45,337
A few of the losing hands

467
00:16:45,337 --> 00:16:47,182
stay up a moment too long,

468
00:16:47,182 --> 00:16:49,738
as if the count might change its mind.

469
00:16:49,738 --> 00:16:52,199
Your last duty is the common chest.

470
00:16:52,199 --> 00:16:54,641
You divide it share by share,

471
00:16:54,641 --> 00:16:57,752
in front of everyone, the way you were elected to do.

472
00:16:58,255 --> 00:17:00,115
The captain gets two shares.

473
00:17:00,115 --> 00:17:01,655
You get two shares.

474
00:17:02,094 --> 00:17:04,178
Tom checks your arithmetic twice.

475
00:17:04,178 --> 00:17:06,298
And history keeps its own count.

476
00:17:06,298 --> 00:17:09,236
Many who took the pardon went back to sea.

477
00:17:09,884 --> 00:17:11,490
Quartermasters who stayed in the

478
00:17:11,490 --> 00:17:13,176
trade went on boarding first and

479
00:17:13,176 --> 00:17:14,170
holding the plunder,

480
00:17:14,170 --> 00:17:17,304
which was exactly the record a court would read aloud.

481
00:17:17,304 --> 00:17:18,678
So why was being a pirate

482
00:17:18,678 --> 00:17:21,127
quartermaster worse than you think?

483
00:17:21,127 --> 00:17:23,356
Because you went first into every boat.

484
00:17:23,356 --> 00:17:25,416
Because you held everyone's

485
00:17:25,416 --> 00:17:27,192
money and nobody's trust for long.

486
00:17:27,192 --> 00:17:29,332
Because you judged your friends,

487
00:17:29,332 --> 00:17:30,520
and then a court judged

488
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:32,104
you by the votes they gave you.

489
00:17:32,104 --> 00:17:33,928
And because the fairness you guarded

490
00:17:33,928 --> 00:17:36,086
so carefully stopped at your own rail.

491
00:17:36,702 --> 00:17:38,848
The sailors you robbed had no vote.

492
00:17:38,848 --> 00:17:41,011
The enslaved people aboard those

493
00:17:41,011 --> 00:17:43,419
prizes were never anyone's share.

494
00:17:43,419 --> 00:17:44,931
A pardon could forgive

495
00:17:44,931 --> 00:17:46,349
a crime against the king.

496
00:17:46,349 --> 00:17:48,585
It could not give back what was taken.

497
00:17:48,585 --> 00:17:51,648
If you want another life behind the legends,

498
00:17:51,648 --> 00:17:52,859
and another job that

499
00:17:52,859 --> 00:17:55,350
sounds better than it was, subscribe.

500
00:17:55,740 --> 00:17:57,220
This is Pirate Pages:

501
00:17:57,220 --> 00:17:59,639
the lives behind the legends.
